<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/foundational/walk-through-western-art">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Art history is not a parade of genius portraits — it is the record of what different eras
    <em>needed images to do</em>: teach the illiterate, glorify patrons, see accurately, express the
    inner life, question the market itself. That is why the discipline asks five recurring questions
    of every work, and this lesson's walk through eleven works applies all five: the
    <strong>cultural context</strong> (what was happening in that society), the
    <strong>interactions across cultures</strong> (what ideas, objects and pigments travelled to make
    it possible), the <strong>materials and techniques</strong> (what the object is physically, and
    what its making made possible), the <strong>purpose and audience</strong> (who paid, who looked,
    what it was for), and the <strong>theories and interpretations</strong> (what it has meant since).
    Follow the eleven works on the timeline and the Western tradition outlines itself: <em>what
    changed, and what changed it</em>.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="six centuries, eleven works — scrub through the shifts" widget="timeline" config={artTimeline} />

  <h2>Cultural contexts: what images were for</h2>
  <p>
    Start where the timeline starts. Giotto's Arena Chapel frescoes (Padua, c. 1305) belong to a
    world where <em>images were the church's literacy</em> — most viewers could not read, so biblical
    narrative had to be legible at a glance. What Giotto added was weight: figures with volume,
    gestures with feeling, a space the eye could walk into — the first step out of flat medieval
    convention toward observed humanity. The Renaissance that followed (Leonardo's <em>Mona Lisa</em>,
    begun 1503; Michelangelo's Sistine ceiling, unveiled 1512) is inseparable from its context:
    <strong>humanism</strong> re-centred attention on the human person, the recovery of classical
    antiquity supplied models, and the great courts and church offices of Italy paid for the result —
    the Sistine ceiling alone covers over 5,000 square feet of vault, painted mostly by one man on
    scaffolding he designed, with the body itself becoming theology. The <strong>Counter-Reformation</strong>
    then demanded that art persuade: after the Council of Trent (1563) ruled that religious images
    should teach clearly and move the viewer, Caravaggio's answer (c. 1600) was the Baroque — violent
    spotlight, ordinary models, saints with dirty feet; realism as recruitment. Meanwhile the
    <strong>Dutch Republic</strong> invented a new audience: no king, no church commission, but
    merchants buying pictures for their homes — which is why Rembrandt's <em>Night Watch</em> (1642)
    is a militia group portrait turned into moving theatre: light as narrative for a paying civic
    client, not an altar.
  </p>

  <h2>Interactions: what travelled to make the art possible</h2>
  <p>
    No work on this timeline is culturally self-made. The <strong>materials travelled first</strong>:
    ultramarine, the Renaissance's most precious blue, was ground from lapis lazuli mined in a single
    Afghan valley and hauled overland to Venice — more expensive than gold, which is why it was
    reserved for the Virgin's robes (patronage written in pigment). Oil painting itself — perfected
    by Jan van Eyck in the early 1400s — spread along trade routes from Flanders into Italy within a
    generation. Ideas travelled too: <strong>Japonisme</strong> — Japanese woodblock prints arriving
    in Europe after Japan opened its ports (1850s) — reset European composition with flat colour
    planes, cropped views and expressive line; Monet owned dozens of Hokusai and Hiroshige prints,
    and Van Gogh copied them openly. The starkest case is <strong>Picasso's <em>Les Demoiselles
    d'Avignon</em></strong> (1907): the shattered planes and mask-like faces owe a documented debt to
    African and Iberian carvings — Picasso had seen African masks at the Trocadéro — a colonial-era
    appropriation that modern scholarship examines from both sides, as the spark of Cubism
    <em>and</em> as Europe consuming other cultures' sacred objects as raw material. The colonial
    dimension runs through the whole story, from the silver of Potosí funding Spanish churches to
    the Latin American modernists (Rivera, Kahlo, Lam) who reworked European painting with
    Indigenous and Afro-Atlantic content. "Interactions within and across cultures" is not a
    footnote; it is how the objects got made.
  </p>

  <h2>Materials, processes, techniques: the medium is an idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every stylistic shift above has a technical enabler. <strong>Egg tempera</strong> (the medieval
    standard) dries in minutes — fine for crisp icons, hopeless for subtlety. <strong>Oil paint</strong>
    dries slowly and can be reworked, which invented the painter's ability to revise: glazes built
    luminosity layer by layer (the Mona Lisa's <strong>sfumato</strong> — edges dissolved in smoke —
    is dozens of translucent veils, technically impossible in tempera). <strong>Fresco</strong>
    (pigment into wet plaster) gave Giotto and Michelangelo permanence and a hard deadline (the
    <em>giornata</em>: one day's plaster, one day's painting). The 19th century industrialised the
    materials: <strong>paint in collapsible tubes</strong> (John Goffe Rand, 1841) took painting out
    of the studio, which is why Impressionism could exist — Monet's <em>Impression, Sunrise</em>
    (1872) is a harbour window painting in minutes, and the critic Louis Leroy's mocking title for
    the group became its name. <strong>Photography</strong> (announced 1839) is the century's
    structural event: it took likeness away from painting, and the "modern" half of the timeline is
    partly a 150-year answer — Impressionism (the eye's instant), Post-Impressionism (Van Gogh's
    <em>Starry Night</em>, 1889: paint as feeling, sky in visible strokes, impasto as emotion),
    Cubism (all sides at once — something a camera cannot do), <strong>Pollock's drips</strong>
    (1947: canvas on the floor, the artist's movement recorded directly, action itself as subject),
    and finally Duchamp's <strong>readymade</strong> — <em>Fountain</em> (1917), a signed urinal,
    which relocates art-making from craft to <em>selection</em>, a technique of ideas.
  </p>

  <h2>Purpose and audience: who paid, who looked</h2>
  <p>
    The patronage chain is the economic spine of the walk: <strong>church → court → merchant →
    museum → market</strong>. Medieval and Renaissance art answered to altarpieces and princes;
    Caravaggio to church rectors with budgets and tastes (his realism was commissioned, not just
    rebelled); Rembrandt to a bourgeois art market the Netherlands invented — the first era where
    ordinary paintings were ordinary commodities. The 19th century added the <strong>Salon
    system</strong> — the state exhibition whose jury could make or destroy a career — and the
    Impressionists' response was institutional revolt: staging their own independent exhibition
    (1874) outside the Salon, the prototype of the artist-run gallery. The 20th century completed
    the shift to the <strong>museum and the market</strong>: Duchamp's urinal was submitted to (and
    rejected by) an exhibition that claimed to accept anything, which was the point — the artwork's
    frame had become the institution itself; Warhol's <em>Campbell's Soup Cans</em> (1962) put the
    supermarket shelf in the gallery and asked what the difference between consumption and culture
    really is, in an art world now run by dealers, critics and museums rather than popes. Every
    style on the timeline is also an economic story about who paid and why — and reading the patron
    usually explains the picture faster than reading the style.
  </p>

  <h2>Theories and interpretations: how to look</h2>
  <p>
    Interpretation is a method, not a mood. The working sequence: <em>What do I literally see?</em>
    (value, colour, space — the formal layer) → <em>How was it made?</em> (medium and technique are
    ideas: sfumato means mist, impasto means urgency, the readymade means selection) → <em>Who was
    it for?</em> (function and patron) → <em>What did it change?</em> The formal layer has its own
    traditions (Wölfflin's paired concepts: linear versus painterly, flat versus deep); the meaning
    layer has <strong>iconography</strong> — Erwin Panofsky's method of reading subjects and symbols
    (the lily means purity; the skull means mortality — a vocabulary the period's viewers had and we
    must relearn); and the modern layer is critical: since the 1970s, feminist art history
    (Linda Nochlin's 1971 question "Why have there been no great women artists?" — and the answer:
    the institutions, not the talent) and the Guerrilla Girls' audits of museum walls have made
    <em>who is represented and who is omitted</em> a mainstream interpretive question. The canon
    itself is now an object of study: this lesson's eleven works were chosen by centuries of
    collectors, museums and textbooks (Gombrich's <em>The Story of Art</em> among them), and knowing
    that the canon is constructed is not cynicism — it is the permission to keep asking what the
    construction left out.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> art history is not a story of progress toward realism, and the
  Renaissance did not "discover" skill that the Middle Ages lacked. Medieval abstraction was a
  choice serving different purposes (symbols, hierarchy, heaven's geometry) — Giotto's naturalism
  answered a new need, not a new ability. Judging every era by its distance from photographic
  accuracy is the anachronism that the post-1839 half of the timeline exists to cure.</p>

  <h2>The method applied: two works, three centuries apart</h2>
  <p>
    See the five questions do real work on a pairing. Giotto's <em>Lamentation</em> in the Arena
    Chapel (c. 1305) versus Caravaggio's <em>The Entombment of Christ</em> (c. 1602–04) — same
    subject family, different worlds. <em>What do I see?</em> Giotto: a shallow stage, figures in
    simplified volumes, grief expressed through a choreography of bowed backs and outflung hands,
    angels screaming in a patterned sky. Caravaggio: a single raking light, bodies half-swallowed by
    darkness, the dead Christ's arm hanging over the tomb edge into the viewer's space.
    <em>How was it made?</em> Fresco on wet plaster — committed in a day, colour fixed into the
    wall — versus oil on canvas: correctable, glazeable, capable of the spotlight gradients fresco
    cannot hold. <em>Who was it for?</em> A private funerary chapel for a Paduan moneylender
    (Enrico Scrovegni, buying prayer against the sin of usury) versus a Roman church altarpiece in
    the Counter-Reformation's campaign to make faith felt. <em>What changed?</em> Giotto reopened
    the door to observed nature that the Trecento walked through for a century; Caravaggio's
    naturalism and light became the Baroque, exported through followers across Europe. Same
    religion, same grief — but the cultural context, the technique, the patron and the influence
    chain are entirely different, and saying so precisely is what an art-historical argument
    <em>is</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper patterns complete the walk. <strong>Periodisation is a convenience, not a truth:</strong>
    "Renaissance", "Baroque" and "modern" are 18th–20th-century sorting labels with fuzzy edges —
    Caravaggio was painting while Shakespeare wrote, and the "modern" break had precedents in Goya's
    black paintings decades before the Impressionists; the labels are useful scaffolding, and
    artefacts of the view from here. <strong>The centres move:</strong> the timeline's geography
    drifts — Italy, then the Dutch Republic, then Paris, then New York replacing Paris after the
    Second World War (Pollock's drips, 1947, the moment the art capital crossed the Atlantic) — and
    each move tracks money, war and institutions as much as talent. <strong>The questions keep
    extending:</strong> Duchamp's institutional question (is art what museums say it is?) has never
    closed — it runs through conceptual art, land art, and today's debates about AI images (whose
    selection? whose training data?) — and the "modern" conversation about what to do once
    photography took likeness away is, on close inspection, still running: every new reproductive
    technology re-asks what painting, or sculpture, or an image is <em>for</em>. That is the deepest
    use of the four-question method from the last section: it works on any object in any century,
    because it asks what the culture needed, what travelled, what the hand did, who paid, and what
    changed — five questions, eleven works, six centuries, one discipline.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gombrich, The Story of Art; Panofsky, Studies in Iconology (1939); Nochlin, "Why Have There Been No Great Women Artists?" (1971); standard museum scholarship (Met, MoMA, Uffizi object histories); Baxandall, Painting and Experience in Fifteenth-Century Italy (1972).</p>
